= Solution
{parent=2}

The <absolute Frobenius morphism> is the identity on the underlying <topological space> and acts on the <structure sheaf> by $a\mapsto a^p$. On an <affine scheme> it is induced by the <ring homomorphism> $A\to A$, $a\mapsto a^p$. This is additive in characteristic $p$, and the inverse image of a <prime ideal> $\mathfrak p$ is again $\mathfrak p$, since $a^p\in\mathfrak p$ exactly when $a\in\mathfrak p$. These affine maps agree under <localization> and hence glue.

A <perfect ring of characteristic p> is one whose <Frobenius endomorphism> is bijective. Since the <absolute Frobenius morphism> fixes points, its map on the <stalk> at $x$ is precisely the <Frobenius endomorphism> of $\mathcal O_{X,x}$. A morphism of sheaves is an <isomorphism> exactly when it is an <isomorphism> on all <stalks>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F_X\text{ is an isomorphism}\iff \mathcal O_{X,x}\text{ is perfect for every }x.}
$$
In the reverse direction the inverse <sheaf> map, together with the identity on points, gives the inverse <morphism of locally ringed spaces>. This is perfection in the Frobenius sense, not the unrelated homological use of the word perfect.

For the <function field> assertions below, interpret the <normal schemes> as integral, as is implicit in writing a single <field> $k(X)$ or $k(Y)$. General <normal schemes> need not be irreducible; without this interpretation those <fields> are not defined globally as single <fields>. Set $K=k(Y)\subseteq L=k(X)$. For an affine open $V=\operatorname{Spec}A$ in $Y$, finiteness gives $f^{-1}V=\operatorname{Spec}B$, with $A\subseteq B$ finite and both <rings> normal domains. In fact $B$ is the <integral closure> of $A$ in $L$: any element of $L$ integral over $A$ is also integral over $B$ and therefore belongs to $B$. This local description yields the two forms of <Frobenius factorization through a finite normal cover>.

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume $L^p\subseteq K$. For $b\in B$, its power $b^p$ belongs to $K$ and is integral over $A$, because $B$ is finite over $A$. Because $A$ is an <integrally closed domain>, $b^p\in A$. Thus
$$
\gamma_V:B\longrightarrow A,\qquad b\longmapsto b^p
$$
is a <ring homomorphism>, defining a morphism $g_V:V\to f^{-1}V$. Its composition with $f:f^{-1}V\to V$ is the <absolute Frobenius morphism> of $f^{-1}V$, because the composite <ring> map sends $b$ to $b^p$ in $B$.

These local morphisms glue. Explicitly, over a distinguished open $D(a)\subseteq V$, the <ring> rule is
$$
\gamma_{D(a)}\left(\frac{b}{a^n}\right)=\frac{b^p}{a^{np}},
$$
so it is exactly the <localization> of $\gamma_V$. For the underlying topology, each fibre of $f$ consists of a single point: if primes $\mathfrak q_1,\mathfrak q_2\subset B$ contract to the same $\mathfrak p$, then
$$
b\in\mathfrak q_j\iff b^p\in\mathfrak p,
$$
so $\mathfrak q_1=\mathfrak q_2$. The map $g_V$ sends that point of $V$ back to its unique point above it. Thus <localization> compatibility on a <basis of a topology> also gives compatibility on overlaps of arbitrary <affine charts>. We obtain
$$
\boxed{g:Y\longrightarrow X,\qquad g\circ f=F_X.}
$$

For uniqueness, $f$ is surjective on points, so the equation determines the point map of $g$. On each <affine chart> it then requires $f^*(g^*(b))=b^p$. The inclusion $A\hookrightarrow B$ is injective, so this determines $g^*(b)$ uniquely as $b^p$. Hence the <sheaf> map and the morphism are unique.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $K\subseteq L^p$. Every $a\in A$ has a unique $p$th root $r(a)$ in $L$: existence is the <field> inclusion and uniqueness follows from injectivity of the <Frobenius endomorphism> in a <field>. Since $r(a)^p=a$, this root satisfies the monic polynomial $T^p-a$ over $B$. Because $B$ is an <integrally closed domain>, this gives $r(a)\in B$. The identities
$$
(r(a)+r(a'))^p=a+a',\qquad (r(a)r(a'))^p=aa'
$$
and uniqueness show that
$$
\eta_V:A\longrightarrow B,\qquad a\longmapsto r(a)
$$
is a <ring homomorphism>.

On $D(a)$ the rule sends a fraction to the fraction of its unique roots. Since $r(a)^p=a$, inverting $a$ is equivalent to inverting $r(a)$, so the rule localizes. Also, for a prime $\mathfrak q\subset B$, $r(a)\in\mathfrak q$ exactly when $a\in\mathfrak q$; hence the induced point map agrees with that of $f$. The localized rules consequently glue to a morphism $h:X\to Y$. Its pullback followed by the <Frobenius endomorphism> sends $a$ to $r(a)^p=a$, which proves
$$
\boxed{f=h\circ F_X.}
$$
If another morphism satisfies this equation, it has the same point map because the <absolute Frobenius morphism> fixes points, and its pullback of $a$ must be a $p$th root of $a$ in the <normal domain> $B$. That root is unique. Thus $h$ is unique.

= 3
{parent=Paper 89}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

A <morphism of schemes> $f:X\to Y$ is a <flat morphism> when each <local ring> $\mathcal O_{X,x}$ is a <flat module> over $\mathcal O_{Y,f(x)}$: tensoring with it preserves <exact sequences>. An <open immersion> induces isomorphisms on the <local rings> at its points, so it is flat.

For a <closed immersion>, the printed equivalence is valid with the additional hypothesis of finite presentation, for example over a locally Noetherian target, and with nonempty source. Here is the proof with that hypothesis. On an <affine chart> the map is $A\to A/I$, with $I$ finitely generated. If $\mathfrak p\supseteq I$, the flat cyclic <module> $A_{\mathfrak p}/I_{\mathfrak p}$ is free over the <local ring> $A_{\mathfrak p}$, and its residue-field <dimension> is one. Therefore its quotient map from $A_{\mathfrak p}$ is an <isomorphism> and $I_{\mathfrak p}=0$. Finite generation gives a neighbourhood of $\mathfrak p$ on which $I$ vanishes. The closed image $V(I)$ is consequently open. A nonempty <clopen> image in a connected target is the whole target; then $I$ vanishes at every prime and is zero. The immersion is an <isomorphism>. The converse is immediate. This proves the <flat closed immersion of finite presentation> assertion.

Without finite presentation, connectedness alone does not suffice. Take $A=C([0,1],\mathbb R)$ and let $\mathfrak m=\{a:a(0)=0\}$. Let $I$ consist of functions vanishing on some neighbourhood of zero. The map $A\to A_{\mathfrak m}$ has kernel $I$: multiplying such a function by a continuous cutoff equal to one at zero kills it, whereas $ga=0$ with $g(0)\ne0$ forces $a$ to vanish near zero. This map is surjective, because the reciprocal of any denominator nonzero at zero can be extended continuously from a small neighbourhood of zero to the whole interval. Thus
$$
A/I\cong A_{\mathfrak m}.
$$
The map $\operatorname{Spec}(A/I)\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ is both a <closed immersion> and a flat <localization>. Its source is connected because its <ring> is local. Its target is connected because continuous <idempotent> functions on the connected interval can only be the constants zero and one. It is not an <isomorphism>: $I$ contains nonzero functions supported away from zero. This supplies a concrete counterexample to the unrestricted assertion. The kernel is a <pure ideal>; this is the general mechanism behind such examples, as explained in \a[https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04PQ][the Stacks Project's treatment of pure ideals].

For the finite-flat fibre assertion, first note the local algebra fact that a finitely generated <flat module> $M$ over a <local ring> $(A,\mathfrak m)$ is free. Choose generators $m_1,\ldots,m_r$ whose residues form a <basis> of $M/\mathfrak mM$, using <Nakayama's lemma>. If a row $a=(a_1,\ldots,a_r)$ gives a relation $\sum a_im_i=0$, the <equational criterion for flatness> supplies elements $n_j$ and a <matrix> $B=(b_{ij})$ such that $m_i=\sum_jb_{ij}n_j$ and $aB=0$. Write each $n_j$ as a linear combination of the $m_i$, with coefficient <matrix> $C$. Then $m=BCm$. Independence modulo $\mathfrak m$ makes $BC$ congruent to the identity. This square <matrix> is invertible over the <local ring>, and $aBC=0$ implies $a=0$. Thus the chosen generators are a <basis>. This also justifies the cyclic-module step above; see \a[https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NV#lemma-finite-flat-local][the finite-flat local lemma].

Now let $\mathcal A=f_*\mathcal O_X$ for the finite <flat morphism>. On each <affine chart> this is the finite flat algebra defining its inverse image. Hence $\mathcal A_y$ is free over $\mathcal O_{Y,y}$, of some finite rank $r_y$. Let $\eta$ be the <generic point> of the irreducible target. Localizing that <free module> at $\eta$ gives
$$
\mathcal A_\eta\cong\mathcal O_{Y,\eta}^{\oplus r_y}.
$$
Every point specializes from the same $\eta$, so every $r_y$ equals the fixed rank at $\eta$. This remains valid if the irreducible target is nonreduced: one compares free ranks over its nonzero generic <local ring>, not necessarily over a <field>. The fibre is affine, and
$$
\Gamma(X_y,\mathcal O_{X_y})\cong\mathcal A_y\otimes_{\mathcal O_{Y,y}}\kappa(y).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\dim_{\kappa(y)}\Gamma(X_y,\mathcal O_{X_y})=r_\eta\quad\text{for every }y.}
$$
This is <finite flat rank over an irreducible scheme>. No unjustified replacement of finite flat by finite locally free on an arbitrary base is needed.

For an algebraic curve of finite type over a <field>, its <normalization of an integral scheme> is finite and birational. If flat, the preceding rank is one, since the generic fibre has the same <function field>. Locally its normalization algebra $B$ is therefore a free $A$-module of rank one. The element $1_B$ generates: its image cannot vanish in the one-dimensional fibre algebra, and <Nakayama's lemma> applies. Thus $A\to B$ is an <isomorphism>. Conversely the identity normalization is flat.

The parenthetical definition in the PDF does not explicitly impose finite type. One can prove the conclusion even in that broader setting without assuming a finite normalization. For any <affine chart>, let $A\subseteq B\subseteq\operatorname{Frac}(A)$ describe its <normalization of an integral scheme>. Integrality makes the map on spectra surjective; if $B$ is flat it is faithfully flat. Faithful flatness gives contraction of <ideals>, $vB\cap A=vA$. For $b=u/v\in B$, with $u,v\in A$ and $v\ne0$, one has $u=vb\in vB\cap A=vA$. Thus $u=va$ for some $a\in A$, so $b=a$. Therefore $B=A$ on every chart. We have proved the <flat normalization is an isomorphism> result and, in particular,
$$
\boxed{C'\to C\text{ is flat}\iff C'=C.}
$$

